<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">30 June 1936 · Geneva</p><h1 class="sp-title">Haile Selassie at the League of Nations</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>In October 1935 Fascist Italy invaded Ethiopia, one of the last independent states in Africa and a member of the League of Nations. The League condemned the invasion but imposed only limited sanctions, and Italian forces using aircraft and poison gas took Addis Ababa in May 1936. Driven into exile, Emperor Haile Selassie appeared before the League's assembly in Geneva to appeal for help, while Italian journalists in the gallery jeered.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>I, Haile Selassie I, Emperor of Ethiopia, am here today to claim that justice which is due to my people, and the assistance promised to it eight months ago...</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Address to the Assembly of the League of Nations, Geneva, 30 June 1936.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Appeal_to_The_League_of_Nations" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Days later the League voted to lift its sanctions on Italy, and its failure encouraged further aggression in Europe and Asia. Haile Selassie spent five years in exile in England and returned to Addis Ababa in 1941, after British and Ethiopian forces drove the Italians out. The speech made him an international symbol of resistance to aggression.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-of-ethiopia-1941">Liberation of Ethiopia (1941)</a></div></div></section>
